#{[17592186222371 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice modeling real-world situations using one-variable inequalities. This includes modeling with compound inequalities and cases where the final answer is left in unsimplified form."] [17592186222372 1029 "Similar-triangle problems often appear in geometry and modeling contexts.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice applying similarity rules in diagrams and word problems. We’ll need to recognize angle relationships, set up side ratios correctly, and efficiently solve for unknown lengths or angles."] [17592186222373 1029 "In this topic, we'll explore more complex structures that occur within systems of linear equations. We'll practice recognizing patterns in systems, using substitution, scaling, symmetry, and new variables to find solutions efficiently."] [17592186222374 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice solving systems of linear equations using elimination. This involves multiplying one or both equations by constant factors and then adding them to eliminate one of the variables."] [17592186222375 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice modeling real-world situations using systems of linear equations and then solving them to find a solution to the original problem. We'll mostly use slope-intercept form to construct our linear models."] [17592186222376 1029 "This topic provides practice in modeling and solving real-world problems with systems of linear equations that are readily expressed in standard form."] [17592186222377 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice analyzing the relationship between systems of linear equations and their graphs by identifying systems that correspond to given graphs and interpreting graphs that represent particular systems."] [17592186222378 1029 "Questions about special triangles, especially right and isosceles triangles, appear frequently in geometry and trigonometry problems. By practicing recognizing these triangles and their angle relationships, we can solve these kinds of problems with fluency."] [17592186222379 1029 "Problems about exterior angles of triangles often appear in geometry questions that combine several angle relationships at once.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice recognizing how exterior and interior angles relate to each other, helping us solve multi-step geometry problems."] [17592186222380 1029 "Problems involving multiple connected triangles usually test the ability to identify shared angles, use geometric relationships, and combine information from several figures.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice these multi-triangle setups to strengthen our spatial reasoning."] [17592186222381 1029 "Problems involving connected triangles often require combining facts about both interior and exterior angles to determine unknown measures.\n\nWith consistent practice, we’ll learn to connect multiple geometric relationships to solve connected triangle problems."] [17592186222382 1029 "Similar-figure questions often ask us to use a given scale factor (or infer one from corresponding side pairs) to find a missing length or perimeter.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice reading the correspondence from descriptions or diagrams and setting up proportions to find unknown lengths."] [17592186222383 1029 "Embedded right-triangle problems often hide similar triangles created by altitudes, parallel lines, or split segments.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice spotting those hidden right triangles and setting up the correct ratios to solve problems."] [17592186222384 1029 "We'll now practice working with perpendicular lines. Here, we'll find slopes of lines that are perpendicular to given equations, whether written in slope-intercept form, general form, or represented as vertical lines."] [17592186222385 1029 "This topic gives you the opportunity to practice matching linear equations to their graphs, and vice versa, in both abstract and real-world situations."] [17592186222386 1029 "This topic provides practice in finding equations of lines in the $xy$-plane using slopes and points. This could involve cases where two points are given, a slope and a point, or a description of the relationship between $x$ and $y$."] [17592186222387 1029 "In this topic, we’ll practice finding $x$- and $y$-intercepts from equations in slope-intercept form, including cases that require simplification or use a given slope."] [17592186222388 1029 "In this topic, we'll get more practice working with lines in slope-intercept form. We'll read information from their graphs and use points on a line to find missing coordinates."] [17592186222389 1029 "In this topic, we'll focus on equations of lines written in standard or general form, finding slopes, intercepts, and equations that match their graphs."] [17592186222390 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice analyzing equations of lines that have been shifted."] [17592186222391 1029 "In this topic, we'll continue analyzing linear equations written in general form. This includes determining coefficients using given points, identifying graphs that satisfy equation restrictions, and interpreting constant terms."] [17592186222392 1029 "Questions about consecutive angles often appear in geometry problems involving parallel lines, polygons, and transversals, where recognizing supplementary relationships quickly can save time.\n\nBy practicing these patterns until they become second nature, we’ll strengthen our geometric reasoning and improve test efficiency."] [17592186222393 1029 "Questions about vertical angles often appear in geometry problems where two or more lines intersect, testing whether we can quickly recognize which angles are equal and apply that fact to find unknown measures.\n\nWith a bit of practice, we can spot these angle relationships almost instantly, saving time and avoiding mistakes."] [17592186222394 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, corresponding angles often appear in multi-step geometry problems involving parallel lines, transversals, and algebraic expressions.\n\nPracticing how to connect these relationships, especially when they combine with supplementary or triangle angle rules, will help us solve geometry problems efficiently."] [17592186222395 1029 "On the math section of the SAT, you may be asked to determine how many solutions a system of two or more linear equations has.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice identifying whether a system has one solution, no solution, or infinitely many solutions by analyzing the equations themselves or their corresponding graphs."] [17592186222396 1029 "In this topic, we'll analyze systems of linear equations with no solution by determining the parameter values that yield parallel lines and inconsistent equations."] [17592186222397 1029 "This topic focuses on equations of perpendicular lines. Here, we’ll write perpendicular lines from general form equations, find coefficient ratios, and identify pairs of perpendicular lines."] [17592186222398 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice modeling in-context problems with systems of linear inequalities, and we'll practice solving for optimal solutions that meet multiple constraints. We'll need to use tools like Desmos to find the feasible regions and determine precise or integer solutions for all these questions."] [17592186222399 1029 "This topic provides practice in analyzing linear equations with no solutions, We'll determine the values of unknown parameters that make the two sides of an equation inconsistent or represent the parallel lines."] [17592186222400 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, you may be asked to interpret the meaning of linear graphs in context. Here, we'll practice identifying unit rates, analyzing totals, and understanding the meaning of $x$-intercepts in real-world situations."] [17592186222401 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, you may encounter systems of linear equations whose solutions can be expressed using parameters. To prepare, we'll go over writing systems in different parametric forms and determine which parametrizations in a given list are valid."] [17592186222402 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, we may see right-triangle problems where side lengths are written with radicals, and we’re expected to simplify and compare the roots.\n\nWith focused practice on factoring radicands and extracting perfect squares, we can avoid common mistakes with radical arithmetic."] [17592186222403 1029 "Circle questions often involve hidden isosceles triangles formed by two radii and a chord. With practice, we’ll learn to quickly spot these triangles and use them to solve problems efficiently."] [17592186222404 1029 "Similar-shape questions often require us to relate scale factors to corresponding changes in area.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice turning length comparisons into area ratios,"] [17592186222405 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice spotting scale factors of three-dimensional shapes and converting cleanly between length, area, and volume proportionality coefficients."] [17592186222406 1029 "Questions about composite solids usually ask to find the surface area after two or more prisms are joined, which means carefully accounting for hidden or overlapping faces and keeping track of units.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice identifying which faces are hidden when solids are glued together."] [17592186222407 1029 "Questions about congruent triangles often test whether we can match corresponding sides and angles using a particular congruence criterion.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice spotting congruence, using angle-sum facts to find unknowns, and transferring equal parts to solve for targeted angles and sides."] [17592186222408 1029 "Triangle similarity is frequently tested by quickly recognizing the AA, SAS, and SSS criteria from diagrams or descriptions.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice determining the minimum information required to guarantee similarity."] [17592186222409 1029 "Chord bisector problems test the ability to connect circle geometry with the Pythagorean theorem and symmetric reasoning.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice using these properties to find missing measures in circle diagrams."] [17592186222410 1029 "The **Wronskian** $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nis defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}x_{11}(t) & x_{12}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t) & x_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nAnalogous to scalar ordinary differential equations (ODEs), the Wronskian of vector-valued functions can be used to determine linear dependency. In particular:\n\n> - If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are *linearly independent*.\n> - If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are *linearly dependent*.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples."] [17592186222411 1029 "In this topic, we'll learn how to compute the **matrix exponential**.\n\nRecall that the scalar exponential function $e^t$ can be written as the power series\n\n$$\ne^t = \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{t^k}{k!} = 1 + t + \\dfrac{t^2}{2} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{t^k}{k!} + \\cdots\n$$\n\nNow, let $A$ be an $n\\times n$ matrix. By definition, the matrix exponential $e^{At}$ is given by the analogous series\n\n$$\ne^{At} = \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{A^k t^k}{k!} = I + tA + \\dfrac{t^2}{2}A^2 + \\cdots + \\dfrac{t^k}{k!}A^k + \\cdots\n$$\n\nThere are several approaches for computing matrix exponentials:\n\n- The most direct approach is to use the definition above, but this is typically impractical.\n- Another method, often covered in linear algebra courses, is to use the Jordan canonical form of $A$. This works, but it can also be tedious, especially for larger matrices.\n\nHere, we will introduce a different method.\n\nBy the Cayley–Hamilton theorem, every power $A^n, A^{n+1}, \\ldots$ can be rewritten as a linear combination of\n$I, \\: A, \\: A^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: A^{n-1}$.\n\nAs a result, $e^{At}$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\ne^{At}=\\alpha_{n-1}A^{n-1} t^{n-1}+\\alpha_{n-2} A^{n-2}t^{n-2}+\\cdots+\\alpha_1 At+\\alpha_0 I\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_0, \\alpha_1, \\ldots, \\alpha_{n-1}$ are functions of $t$ and $I$ is the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nNow, we define the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda)=\\alpha_{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1}+\\alpha_{n-2}\\lambda^{n-2}+\\cdots+\\alpha_1\\lambda+\\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThen we have the following key fact:\n\n> For any eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of $At$, we have $r(\\lambda) = e^{\\lambda}$.\n\nLet’s now see how this method works in practice."] [17592186222412 1029 "Suppose we wish to approximate a solution to an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nRecall that Euler's method approximates a solution by evaluating the slope at the current point:\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nAlthough this method is simple, it's not too accurate and can be unstable for some problems, meaning errors compound quickly.\nOne alternative is the implicit Euler method, which instead evaluates the slope at the *next* point, where\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nThat is, the **implicit Euler method** (or **backward Euler method**) with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nBut how can we evaluate the slope at the next point if we need to know the slope to find the coordinates of the next point?\n\nWell, since $y_\\text{new}$ is found by adding $\\Delta y$ to $y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + f(x_{new}, y_{new}) \\cdot Δx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $y_\\text{new}$ appears on both sides. This makes the update implicit: we solve for $y_\\text{new}$ directly, rather than explicitly calculating the value of $\\Delta y$ beforehand!\n\nIn this lesson, we'll focus on IVPs whose slope function is linear in $y$, i.e., of the form\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = a(x)y + b(x)\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we can simply solve the implicit equation for $y_\\text{new}$, as demonstrated in the following worked example.\n\nFor more complex slope functions, we require a root-finding method, such as Newton's method to solve the implicit equation iteratively. We'll cover this in a later lesson."] [17592186222413 1029 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x-y+4, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nLet's use the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate the value of $y(1)$.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1\n$$\n\nThen, in general, the change $\\Delta y$ for our ODE is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (x_{new} - y_{new} + 4) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= x_{new} - y_{new} + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand solve for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + x_{new} - y_{new} + 4 \\\\\n2y_{new} &= y + x_{new} + 4 \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{2}(y + x_{new} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain a general formula for the new $y$ value, $y_\\text{new}$, in terms of the new value $x_\\text{new}$ and the current value $y$ - all values we know at any given step!\n\nPlugging in our known values\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + 1 = 1\n$$\n\nand $y = 1$, we get a new $y$-value of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{2}(1 + 1 + 4) = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx 3\n$$\n\nWe can view our approximation made by the implicit Euler method in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6396/Source/Images/021675b756f13cfb90d322b910cfe2f6.png)"] [17592186222414 1029 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to compute Laplace transforms. We now examine how the Laplace transform changes when a function is multiplied by an exponential.\n\nSuppose\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nexists for $s>s_0$. We want to compute the Laplace transform of $e^{at}f(t)$.\n\nStarting from the definition of the Laplace transform,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{at}f(t)} &= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}e^{at}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{- (s - a)t}f(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t, \\qquad s>s_0\n$$\n\nComparing the two integrals, we see that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t =F(s-a)\n$$\n\nFor this to make sense, we need $s-a>s_0$, which is equivalent to\n$s>s_0+a$.\n\nThis result is known as the **First Shifting Theorem**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}f(t)\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nIn the next slide, we will apply this result to a concrete example."] [17592186222415 1029 "From the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\} =\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st}u(t-a)g(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nBecause $u(t-a)=0$ for $t < a$ and $u(t-a)=1$ for\n\n$$\nt\\ge a\n$$\n\n, the integrand is zero until $t=a$.\nSo the integral reduces to\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\} =\\int_a^\\infty e^{-st}g(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nTo compare this with a standard Laplace transform, we shift the variable of integration so that the lower limit is $0$.\n\nLet $\\tau=t-a$. Then\n\n$$\nt=\\tau+a\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}t=\\textrm{d}\\tau\n$$\n\n. Also, when $t=a$, we have $\\tau=0$.\n\nSubstituting gives\n\n$$\n\\int_a^\\infty e^{-st}g(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t =\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s(\\tau+a)}g(\\tau+a)\\,\\textrm{d}\\tau\n$$\n\nNow separate the exponential term:\n\n$$\ne^{-s(\\tau+a)}=e^{-as}e^{-s\\tau}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to factor out the constant $e^{-as}$:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s(\\tau+a)}g(\\tau+a)\\,\\textrm{d}\\tau =e^{-as}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s\\tau}g(\\tau+a)\\,\\textrm{d}\\tau\n$$\n\nThe remaining integral has exactly the form of a Laplace transform, so recognize\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s\\tau}g(\\tau+a)\\,\\textrm{d}\\tau =\\mathcal{L}\\{g(t+a)\\}\n$$\n\nPutting everything together, we obtain the **second shifting theorem**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\} =e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{g(t+a)\\}, \\qquad \\text{for} s>s_0\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway is that the unit step shifts the *start time* of the function to $t=a$, and changing variables shifts it back to $0$. The exponential factor $e^{-as}$ records this delay in the $s$-domain."] [17592186222416 1029 "Suppose a quantity increases over time at a rate proportional to its current value, such as the balance of a bank account growing with continuous interest. The Laplace transform can be used to analyze how such exponential growth behaves, especially when the function is **integrated over time** to represent total accumulation.\n\nTo illustrate this, we’ll find the Laplace transform of a function defined as the integral of an exponential function. This shows how *integration in the time domain* corresponds to *division by $s$* in the $s$-domain.\n\nSuppose we are given that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s>a\n$$\n\nLet's find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\int_{0}^{t} e^{\\tau/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau, \\qquad s > \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe apply the **Integration in Time Property**: if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(t)=e^{t/2}\n$$\n\n. Thus, for\n\n$$\ns>\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= L{f(t)} \\\\\n&= L{e^{t/2}} \\\\\n&= (1)/((s - \\frac{1}{2}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have for\n\n$$\ns>\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t)} &= L{∫_{0}^{t}e^{τ/2}dτ} \\\\\n&= (F(s))/(s) \\\\\n&= (1)/(s(s - \\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\n&= (2)/(s(2s - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186222417 1029 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe **$2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton** (**ABM $2$-step**) **Method** with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$,\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$, and\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)$.\n\nThe final approximation for the new step is\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nSo, the corrector of the ABM $2$-step combines the slope evaluated at the predicted point with the slope evaluated at the current point and the previous point. Let's demonstrate how to apply the corrector equation to our previous example."] [17592186222418 1029 "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x-2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe will now complete our approximation of the value of $y(1)$ using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method.\n\nFirst, recall that we previously initialized with the RK4 method and completed the predictor step of the ABM $2$-step method, resulting in the following values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |  |  |\n\nNow, the slope at the point\n\n$$\n(x_\\text{new},y_p) = \\left(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{13}8\\right) = \\left(1, \\dfrac{13}8\\right)\n$$\n\nestimated by the predictor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= 4x_{new} - 2y_{p} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 1 - 2 \\cdot \\frac{13}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac34\\right)$ is $y' = \\dfrac12$.\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2$.\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(5 \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} + 8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} - (-2)) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{24} \\cdot \\frac{39}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{13}{32}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n\nWe know the value of $x$ in the next row is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new}=1\n$$\n\nTo get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac34 + \\dfrac{13}{32} = \\dfrac{37}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\dfrac{37}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx \\dfrac{37}{32}\n$$"] [17592186222419 1029 "Recall that the matrix exponential $e^{At}$ can be written as\n\n$$\ne^{At}=\\alpha_{n-1} A^{n-1}t^{n-1}+\\alpha_{n-2} A^{n-2}t^{n-2}+\\cdots+\\alpha_1 At+\\alpha_0 I\n$$\n\nIf we define the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda)=\\alpha_{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1}+\\alpha_{n-2}\\lambda^{n-2}+\\cdots+\\alpha_1\\lambda+\\alpha_0\n$$\n\nthen for any eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$ of $At$, we have\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda_i) = e^{\\lambda_i}\n$$\n\nHowever, for *repeated eigenvalues*, we have the following additional fact:\n\n> If $\\lambda_i$ is an eigenvalue of multiplicity $k$ with $k>1$, then\n> $\\frac{d}{dλ}r(λ)\\begin{vmatrix}_{λ = λ_{i}} = e^{λ_{i}}, \\frac{d^{2}}{dλ^{2}}r(λ) & _{λ = λ_{i}} = e^{λ_{i}}, …, \\frac{d^{k - 1}}{dλ^{k - 1}}r(λ)\\end{vmatrix}_{λ = λ_{i}} = e^{λ_{i}}$\n\nLet's apply these facts to a concrete example."] [17592186222420 1029 "Let's find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nBy solving the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A-\\lambda I &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe can find that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=2, \\qquad \\lambda_2=7\n$$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2α_{1}t + α_{0} & 0 \\\\ -7α_{1}t & 7α_{1}t + α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=2t, \\qquad \\lambda_2=7t\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{r(2t) = e^{2t}; r(7t) = e^{7t}⇒{2tα_{1} + α_{0} = e^{2t}; 7tα_{1} + α_{0} = e^{7t}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n5t\\alpha_1 = e^{7t} - e^{2t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{5t}\\bigl(e^{7t}-e^{2t}\\bigr)\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $7$ and subtracting twice the second one, we get\n\n$$\n5\\alpha_0 = 7e^{2t} - 2e^{7t} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\bigl(7e^{2t}-2e^{7t}\\bigr)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 2(\\frac{1}{5t}(e^{7t} - e^{2t}))t + \\frac{1}{5}(7e^{2t} - 2e^{7t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}((2e^{7t} - 2e^{2t}) + (7e^{2t} - 2e^{7t})) \\\\\n&= e^{2t} \\\\\n- 7α_{1}t &= -7(\\frac{1}{5t}(e^{7t} - e^{2t}))t \\\\\n&=-\\frac{7}{5}(e^{7t} - e^{2t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{5}(e^{2t} - e^{7t}) \\\\\n7α_{1}t + α_{0} &= 7(\\frac{1}{5t}(e^{7t} - e^{2t}))t + \\frac{1}{5}(7e^{2t} - 2e^{7t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}((7e^{7t} - 7e^{2t}) + (7e^{2t} - 2e^{7t})) \\\\\n&= e^{7t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5e^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 7(e^{2t} - e^{7t}) & 5e^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186222421 1029 "For a first-order initial value problem, we have already seen ways to approximate the increment in $y$ over a step, including:\n\n- the modified Euler method, which predicts using Euler's method and then corrects that prediction, and\n- the RK4 method, which combines several slope evaluations within a single step.\n\nAnother group of methods, called the **Adams-Bashforth-Moulton methods**, takes a different approach. Rather than computing multiple slopes at new points, these methods reuse slopes from previous steps in a predictor-corrector framework, giving high accuracy with relatively little additional work.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll focus on one of the simplest: the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the **$2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method** (**ABM $2$-step**) with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the slope at the current point $(x,y)$, and\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$, where $x_{-1} = x-\\Delta x$.\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by\n\n$$\ny_p = y + \\Delta y_p\n$$\n\nSince ABM $2$-step uses the slope from the previous step, it cannot be started from a single initial condition alone.\nInstead, we initialize the method by computing the first step with a one-step method, typically the RK4 method.\n\nOnce the prediction $y_p$ is computed, we apply a corrector equation to refine our approximation. We'll return to this step shortly.\nBefore that, let's see an example of how to apply the predictor equation."] [17592186222422 1029 "In this topic, we'll study phase portraits of linear systems\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhen the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at least one *zero eigenvalue*.\n\nA zero eigenvalue is special because\n\n$$\ne^{0\\cdot t}=1\n$$\n\nso the corresponding component of a solution has *no exponential growth or decay*. Geometrically, this often creates a **line of equilibrium points** (infinitely many fixed points), instead of a single equilibrium at the origin.\n\nIndeed, equilibrium points satisfy\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nso for\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=A\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwe solve\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThat is, equilibrium points form the *null space* of $A$.\n\n- If $\\det(A) \\neq 0$, then the null space is $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ and the only equilibrium is the origin.\n- If $\\det(A)=0$, then the null space contains infinitely many points (typically a line through the origin in $2\\times2$ cases).\n\nA zero eigenvalue guarantees $\\det(A)=0$, so in that case the null space is nontrivial.\n\nLet's see a concrete example."] [17592186222423 1029 "Where do the predictor and corrector equations of the two-step Adams–Bashforth–Moulton method come from?\n\nThe method consists of two parts: the **Adams-Bashforth** (**AB2**) **predictor**, and **Adams-Moulton** (**AM2**) **corrector**.\nWe'll begin by deriving the AB2 predictor.\n\nConsider an initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a)=c\n$$\n\nWriting the differential equation in integral form, we have\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\Delta y = \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = x_\\text{new} - x\n$$\n\nbe the step size, and define the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t - x}{\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\nt = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n$$\n\nso the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n$$\n\nNow, define a new function $g$ of $\\theta$ as the integrand:\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulas result from approximating $g(\\theta)$ by a simple function that agrees with its known values, and then integrating that approximation from $0$ to $1$. We now determine some known values.\n\n- Evaluating $g$ at $\\theta = 0$, we have\n$g(0)|= f(x + 0 \\cdot Δx, y(x + 0 \\cdot Δx)); = f(x, y); = y^{′}$.\n- Similarly, evaluating $g$ at $\\theta=-1$, we get\n$g(-1)|= f(x + (-1) \\cdot Δx, y(x + (-1) \\cdot Δx)); = f(x - Δx, y(x - Δx)); = f(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})); = y_{-1}^{′}$,\nwhere $x_{-1}$ is the previous $x$-value and $y'_{-1}$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1}))$.\n\nSince we have two known values, the simplest reasonable approximation to $g(\\theta)$ is a *straight line* passing through these two points: $(0,y')$ and $(-1,y'_{-1})$.\nThis straight line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(θ) &= y^{′} + (y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′})/(0 - (-1))(θ - 0) \\\\\n&= y^{′} + (y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′})θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by integrating this approximation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}p(θ)dθ &= ∫_{0}^{1}(y^{′} + (y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′})θ)dθ \\\\\n&= (y^{′}θ + \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′})θ^{2}) \\mid _{θ = 0}^{θ = 1} \\\\\n&= y^{′} + \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\nΔy = ∫_{0}^{1}g(θ)dθ \\cdot Δx \\approx ∫_{0}^{1}p(θ)dθ \\cdot Δx = \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we define as the AB2 predictor:\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x\n$$"] [17592186222424 1029 "So far, we have derived the AB2 predictor. We are left to derive the AM2 corrector.\n\nRecall that we wrote the differential equation in integral form as\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_x^{x_\\text{new}} f(t,y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\Delta y = \\int_x^{x_\\text{new}} f(t,y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nWith the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t-x}{\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nwe had\n\n$$\nt = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x+\\theta\\Delta x,y(x+\\theta\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n$$\n\nand we defined\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x+\\theta\\Delta x,y(x+\\theta\\Delta x))\n$$\n\nFor the predictor, we considered two known values of $g(\\theta){:}$\n\n- For $\\theta = 0$, we have $g(0) = y'$.\n- For $\\theta = -1$, we have $g(-1) = y'_{-1}$.\n\nThen, we approximated $g$ with a linear function passing through these two points.\n\nNow, for the corrector, we use *three* known values, and approximate $g(\\theta)$ by a *quadratic* passing through these three points.\n\nTo obtain a third value, we also want the slope at the *new* $x$-value\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x\n$$\n\nBut, the true value $y_\\text{new}$ is not yet known.\n\nInstead, we evaluate the slope at the new point estimated by the AB2 predictor! This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\theta = 1{:}\n$$\n\n$$\ng(1) = f(x + \\Delta x, y(x + \\Delta x)) \\approx f(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = y'_p\n$$\n\nwhere $y_p$ is the new $y$-value predicted by AB2, and $y'_p$ is the slope evaluated at $(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$.\nSo, our three points are\n\n$$\n(-1,y'_{-1}), \\quad (0,y'), \\quad (1,y'_p)\n$$\n\nLet $q(\\theta)$ be the quadratic that passes through these three points. After simplifying, this quadratic can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq(θ) &= \\frac{1}{2}θ(θ + 1)y_{p}^{′} - (θ - 1)(θ + 1)y^{′} + \\frac{1}{2}θ(θ - 1)y_{-1}^{′} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(θ^{2} + θ)y_{p}^{′} - (θ^{2} - 1)y^{′} + \\frac{1}{2}(θ^{2} - θ)y_{-1}^{′}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate this approximation from $0$ to $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}q(θ)dθ &= ∫_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}(θ^{2} + θ)y_{p}^{′} - (θ^{2} - 1)y^{′} + \\frac{1}{2}(θ^{2} - θ)y_{-1}^{′})dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{2})y_{p}^{′} - (\\frac{1}{3} - 1)y^{′} + \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{2})y_{-1}^{′} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{12}y_{p}^{′} + \\frac{2}{3}y^{′} - \\frac{1}{12}y_{-1}^{′} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 g(\\theta) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x \\approx \\int_0^1 q(\\theta) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we use as the AM2 corrector. Putting it together, the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186222425 1029 "Now, suppose a $2 \\times 2$ system has a repeated zero eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (multiplicity $2$). Here, the vector field carries no exponential growth or decay in any direction.\nThere are two subcases.\n\n- If there are two independent eigenvectors (we have a zero matrix), then every direction behaves like an equilibrium direction, and solutions are constant:\n$\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(0)$.\nSo *every point is an equilibrium*, and the phase portrait is a field of zero vectors.\n- If there is only one eigenvector (we have a singular nonzero matrix), then we use a generalized eigenvector $\\mathbf{w}$ with $(A-0\\cdot I)\\mathbf{w}=A\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{v}$, and solutions look like\n$\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{v}+c_2\\bigl(t\\,\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w}\\bigr), \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\nNotice that here$\\mathbf{x}_E=c_1\\mathbf{v}$ still gives a *line of equilibrium points*, whileother trajectories align with the eigenspace structure, yielding a **shear flow** where solutions grow linearly with $t$ (algebraic growth).\n\nLet's see a concrete example of the second case."] [17592186222426 1029 "Suppose a $2 \\times 2$ system has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=0$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwith eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$.\n\nThen, the eigenvector direction for $\\lambda_1=0$ gives a **line of equilibria**:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_E = c_1 \\mathbf{v}_1, \\qquad c_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nThe other eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$ determines what happens off the equilibrium line:\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0$, trajectories move *away* from the equilibrium line (*unstable* direction).\n- If $\\lambda_2<0$, trajectories move *toward* the equilibrium line (*stable* direction).\n\nA typical solution has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + c_2 e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nSo trajectories are *parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2$,* shifted by the equilibrium vector $c_1\\mathbf{v}_1$.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) = x(t) - 2y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) + 2y(t)\n$$\n\nwhere we know the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=3\n$$\n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ respectively.\n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- First, notice that $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}$. These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/d9a0cb6aea063e7d938b9218221c580a.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= -x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since $e^{3t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/797029119444de0f10795c0a00694204.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$. So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/f8bc061c5ebd1d33bfc1892a1daad387.png)"] [17592186222427 1029 "Can we apply Euler's method to approximate solutions to second-order initial value problems, such as the one below?\n\n$$\nxy'' - y' - y = 2x, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\quad y'(1) = 1\n$$\n\nWell, for a second-order initial value problem,\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x, y, y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(a) = c, \\\\ z^{′} = f(x, y, z),\\end{vmatrix}z(a) = d\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation:\n\n$$\n{Δy = y^{′} \\cdot Δx; Δz = z^{′} \\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z\n$$\n\nIn the example above, we set up Euler's method by first isolating $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxy^{″} - y^{′} - y &= 2x \\\\\nxy^{″} &= y + y^{′} + 2x \\\\\ny^{″} &= \\frac{y + y^{′}}{x} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z=y'$, $z'=y''$ in the above. This gives the system\n\n$$\n{y^{′} = z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(1) =-1 \\\\ z^{′} = \\frac{y + z}{x} + 2,\\end{vmatrix}z(1) = 1\n$$\n\nOn the next slide, we will approximate values for this system using step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac12\n$$"] [17592186222428 1029 "Recall that for\n\n$$\nx'(t) = f(x)\n$$\n\na point $x^\\ast$ is an *equilibrium* if\n\n$$\nf(x^\\ast) = 0\n$$\n\nSolutions starting at $x^\\ast$ remain constant for all $t$.\n\nNow, consider a system in the plane with variables $x(t)$ and $y(t){:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f_{1}(x, y) \\\\ f_{2}(x, y)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nA point\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^\\ast = (x^\\ast, y^\\ast)\n$$\n\nis an **equilibrium point** if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast) = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nBoth components must be zero:\n\n$$\n{f_{1}(x^{∗}, y^{∗}) = 0; f_{2}(x^{∗}, y^{∗}) = 0\n$$\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast\n$$\n\nfor all $t$. The state does not change.\n\n**Note:** In the phase plane, $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ represents velocity. At an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$, the velocity is $\\mathbf{0}$, so trajectories starting there stay there forever.\n\nFor example, let's find the equilibrium points of the following system:\n\n$$\n{x^{′} = x - y; y^{′} = x + y - 2\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** Set the right-hand sides equal to zero.\n${x - y = 0; x + y - 2 = 0$\n- **Step 2:** Solve the system. From the first equation, $y = x$. Substitute into the second:\n$x + (x) - 2 = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 2x = 2 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x = 1$.\nThus, $y = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the system has a single equilibrium point $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186222429 1029 "So far, we have learned how to find equilibrium points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)\n$$\n\nHowever, just knowing where equilibrium points are is not enough: we also want to understand *how nearby solutions behave* when we start close to an equilibrium point.\n\nRoughly speaking, there are three qualitatively different behaviors.\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **asymptotically stable** if there exists a neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty$.In plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6377/Source/Images/fcf886271f3962652c4152d853846290.png)\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **stable** if for any neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ there exists a neighborhood $O_1 \\subseteq O$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O_1$ at some point in time $t=t_0$ remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0$.In plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6377/Source/Images/20882d729e728c045888951d510dda40.png)\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **unstable** if it is not stable.In plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6377/Source/Images/d33fd67ff7aff6c50d538f0ec3774d3c.png)\n\n**Watch out!** These descriptions are *local*: they talk about what happens when the initial condition is *sufficiently close* to the equilibrium. They do not require that all trajectories in the entire plane behave the same way.\n\nNext, let's see some examples."] [17592186222430 1029 "We have seen how, for an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates a solution by evaluating the slope at the current point,\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhile the implicit Euler method approximates a solution by evaluating the slope at the next point,\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nBoth have their advantages, but is there a way to combine them into one numerical method, applying the slope at both the current point and next point to update $y$?\n\nYes, there is! One such method is the trapezoidal method, which takes the *average slope in the interval between $x$ and $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x$.*\n\nMore formally, the **trapezoidal method** with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nLike the implicit Euler method, the trapezoidal method is an implicit numerical method. To apply the method, we substitute the formula for $\\Delta y$ into the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{2}(f(x, y) + f(x_{new}, y_{new})) \\cdot Δx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve the resulting implicit equation for $y_\\text{new}$ directly, rather than explicitly calculating the value of $\\Delta y$ beforehand.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll focus on IVPs whose slope function is linear in $y$, i.e., of the form\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = a(x)y + b(x)\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we can simply solve the implicit equation for $y_\\text{new}$, as demonstrated in the following worked example.\n\nFor more complex slope functions, we require a root-finding method, such as Newton's method, to solve the implicit equation iteratively. We'll cover this in a later lesson."] [17592186222431 1029 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 2\n$$\n\nLet's use the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate the value of $y(1)$.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1\n$$\n\nThen, in general, the change $\\Delta y$ for our ODE is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(x + y + 1 + (x_{new} + y_{new} + 1)) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(x + x_{new} + y + y_{new} + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{2}(x + x_{new} + y + y_{new} + 2) \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{2}(x + x_{new} + 3y + y_{new} + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{2}(x + x_{new} + 3y + 2) + \\frac{1}{2}y_{new} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}y_{new} &= \\frac{1}{2}(x + x_{new} + 3y + 2) \\\\\ny_{new} &= x + x_{new} + 3y + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = 0 + 1 = 1\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = 0 + 1 + 3\\cdot2 + 2= 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx 9\n$$\n\nWe can view our approximation made by the trapezoidal method in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6373/Source/Images/9774dbd9d47d2989cffb4249665fd243.png)"] [17592186222432 1029 "Consider an autonomous system written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}f_{1}(x, y) \\\\ f_{2}(x, y)\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThe system is called **almost linear** at an equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ if the Jacobian determinant at this point is nonzero there, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\det\\!\\bigl(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\\bigr)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nFor example, let\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x + y + y^{2} \\\\ x + y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nso $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium.\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y + y^{2}) = 1, & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + y + y^{2}) = 1 + 2y \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y) = 1, & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + y) = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 + 2y \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ(0, 0) &= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 + 2(0) \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian is $0$, the system is *not* almost linear at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$."] [17592186222433 1029 "Again, suppose $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium of the nonlinear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nand assume the system is *almost linear* at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. If\n\n$$\nJ=\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\n$$\n\ndenotes the Jacobian matrix evaluated at the equilibrium, then the behavior of solutions near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is determined by the eigenvalues of $J$.\n\nThis information can be summarized in the following table.\n\n| **Eigenvalues of the Jacobian** | **Behavior** | **Stability** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| real and both positive | source / unstable node | unstable |\n| real and both negative | sink / stable node | asymptotically stable |\n| real and opposite signs | saddle | unstable |\n| complex with positive real part | spiral source | unstable |\n| complex with negative real part | spiral sink | asymptotically stable |\n\nLet's now consider a concrete example."] [17592186222434 1029 "Suppose the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nis almost linear at an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. In this case, the system's behavior near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ can be approximated by a linear system.\n\nFirst, we define the deviation from equilibrium by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x}^\\ast\n$$\n\nSmall values of $\\mathbf{u}$ correspond to points close to the equilibrium.\n\nNext, assuming\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = [x, y]^T\n$$\n\nwe compute the Jacobian matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand evaluate it at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to obtain the constant matrix\n\n$$\nJ = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nthe nonlinear system can be approximated by the **linearized system**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}'= J \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nThis linear system captures the leading-order behavior of solutions close to the equilibrium.\n\nLet's see an example."]}
